foreign  [Music]  for Tuesday October 18th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  if you live in California you might have  seen this commercial featuring Governor  Gavin Newsom fellow Californians I need  to warn you about proposition 30. one  company cynical scheme to grab a huge  taxpayer-funded subsidy the company the  governor is talking about is the ride  hailing giant lift and the ballot  measure that Lyft supports proposition  30 would raise taxes on high income  residents specifically an additional  1.75 tax on income over 2 million  dollars for the next 20 years that would  then be used to fund the transition to  Greener forms of transportation as well  as fund Wildfire prevention and lift and  prop 30's other supporters are running  ads too California has the worst air  pollution in the country  the top two causes are vehicles and  wildfires prop 30 helps clean our air  now the positions the two sides are  taking might seem a bit strange Governor  Newsom has supported some of the  strictest environmental laws in the  country and Lyft is a big corporation  whose business relies on driving so  what's behind this strange Division and  what will it mean for the ballot  measures chances  joining us to discuss that is our Tech  reporter pritik Arana hi pritika thanks  for joining us Hey Zoe it's great to be  back on the show so can you start us off  by you know telling us why Lyft says  it's supporting this ballot measure prop  30. right so you know lyft's co-founder  and CEO Logan green wrote quite a  compelling blog post last month  explaining why the company was  supporting the measure and he says that  as a father of four what he sees in  California really bothers him and he  says he sees record setting wildfires  plaguing every part of the state  unrelenting drought extreme heat and he  says we urgently need to save the  environment so that's what Liv's  co-founder says but if you take a closer  look analysts say the real reason Lyft  is in this is because California  requires them to convert most of their  vehicles to zero emission vehicle goals  meaning go electric by 2030 and that is  a tall order remember Lyft doesn't own  any of the cars the people driving on  their platform are gig workers so in  order to you know Force these people to  go electric they would need to  incentivize drivers make it cheaper for  drivers who typically tend to be lower  and middle income earners to afford  electric vehicles they would need to  have Chargers across California to make  it easier for these drivers to actually  sustain that shift okay so that's what's  behind maybe lyft's thinking Governor  Gavin Newsom who has been you know a big  supporter of green energy measures in  the past he does not support this ballot  measure can you tell us a little bit  about why he doesn't and why is he so  cynical about lyft's support of it yeah  covered in Newsome surprised many in the  state it when he came out in these ads  and he basically slammed Lyft for  bankrolling this measure now to be clear  both camps support the switch to going  green and they both say we need to do  more to prevent wildfires we need to do  more to reduce carbon emissions we need  to do more to switch to Greener forms of  transportation so both groups agree with  this where they disagree is who should  pay for it now Governor newsom's  argument is that we don't need extra  taxes for something that we're already  doing he says that he has committed 10  billion dollars over the next six years  to build charging stations across the  state to incentivize people to switch to  electric vehicles so we don't actually  need to raise the tax rate which by the  way is already high in California Lyft  says it's a good thing that Governor  Newsom is thinking about this and has a  plan in place but 10 billion over 6  years isn't enough for long-term and  sustainable shift what Lyft and other  environmentalists are proposing is to  collect about 100 billion over the next  20 years and use that money for the long  haul you know this isn't just a fight  between Lyft and Newsome though I mean  who are the other supporters and  opponents of this bill yeah you know  it's brought together  a very unusual set of characters people  who typically wouldn't work with each  other have joined forces so on one side  we have Governor Newsom who's a Democrat  joining with the state's most powerful  teachers union and we also have business  groups and the California Republican  party that is opposing the tax so  Governor Newsom is on the same side as  you know his sworn enemy the Republican  party on the other side we have Governor  newsom's Democratic party  environmentalists and Union groups that  are standing with Lyft so it's been  interesting to see the divisions that  this measure has created what about Uber  where does it stand on this issue  because I imagine it would have some of  the same concerns as Lyft oh absolutely  you know if more money is spent on  incentivizing drivers to switch to  electric vehicles if more money is spent  on charging station  Uber absolutely benefits as well but  they've kind of been silent on this  proposition they haven't joined forces  with Lyft because Lyft has more to lose  their exposure to California is way  greater than Ubers just to give you an  example some 16 of lifts bookings come  from California versus nine percent for  Uber so you can really see that Lyft is  doubling down because even though  California is important for both big  ride share companies it's way more  important for Lyft what kind of money is  backing the campaigns so this is again a  very interesting one the yes Camp which  is environmentalists and Lyft they've  raised about 50 million dollars to  support the measure and what's  interesting is that almost all of that  money has come from Lyft so Lyft has  basically bankrupt rolled more than 95  percent of this campaign on the other  side the opponents have raised just  about 20 million compared to the yes  camps 50 million and that side is being  funded by the California Teachers  Association and a few Silicon Valley  investors and billionaires so some  notable names include Netflix CEO read  Hastings Silicon Valley investor Sam  Altman and another investor and a  partner at Sequoia Capital Michael  Moritz okay so that's all the money  that's all the people who are backing  these kinds of things what are the polls  saying about the chances of this ballot  measure passing you know before Governor  Newsom jumped into The Fray with his  television campaign that was  overwhelming support for this measure so  in July a poll said 63 percent of  Californians were in favor of the  measure in September another poll said  55 percent were in support and just  weeks after Governor newsom's ads began  to run in October we're seeing support  dip below 50 for the first time so now  only 49 of Californians are supporting  this measure so it's going to be a very  close race I guess we'll just have to  watch on Election Day that's a reporter  pritik Arana thanks for joining us thank  you Zoe and that's it for today's Tech  news briefing if you want more Tech  stories check out our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal thanks for listening  [Music]

